Positioning of Graphics Products For AlphaStation Systems

HIGHLIGHTS

*  Full range of graphics now available for AlphaStation workstations
*  Fastest high-end graphics complement fastest workstation to create
   fastest graphics workstation available

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

With the announcement of the ZLXp-L family of graphic accelerators and the
availability of Freedom Series graphics from Evans & Sutherland, Digital now
offers a full range of graphics for its AlphaStation workstations to satisfy
the needs of all users in Mechanical CAD (MCAD), Electrical CAD (ECAD),
geographic information systems (GIS), computer aided software engineering
(CASE), and scientific visualization.

The following article helps position these new products to other graphics
products currently available for AlphaStation systems.

Commodity graphics

Digital offers inexpensive, PC commodity level graphics on all AlphaStation
models. All models support the ATI mach64 on Digital UNIX (formerly DEC
OSF/1), OpenVMS, and Microsoft Windows NT. They also support the DEC 864
graphics adapter, based on technology from S3 corporation, on Microsoft
Windows NT operating systems. These graphics are especially recommended for
CASE and ECAD users whose applications require little graphics horsepower.

High-end 2D

For price-sensitive users in MCAD, ECAD, CASE, and GIS requiring high-end 2D
with entry-level 3D graphics performance, the ZLXp-E family of products is
the solution. The ZLXp-E series consists of three models. The ZLXp-E1 is for
those users who need very fast line drawing or very fast copying of images
from workstation memory to the display. It is the fastest 2D graphics card
available at any price from any vendor and can display 256 colors
simultaneously. The ZLXp-E2 is the high performance graphics card for
displaying images in true color--with the capability of displaying 16.7
million colors simultaneously. The ZLXp-E3 is ideal for cost sensitive
Windows NT users in MCAD and GIS who need entry-performance 3D
smooth-shading as well high-end 2D graphics and 3D wireframe capabilities.
Digital UNIX and OpenVMS users should purchase the ZLXp-L1 graphics,
described below, in place of the ZLXp-E3.

Midrange 3D

For MCAD, GIS, and scientific visualization users requiring solid
midrange-performance 3D smooth-shaded graphics acceleration, the new ZLXp-L
graphics accelerators are the solution. The ZLXp-L series consists of two
models. The ZLXp-L1 is an excellent value for most of the
midrange-performance 3D requirements of MCAD and GIS, providing outstanding
value compared to HP's and SGI's similarly priced graphics options. The
ZLXp-L2 is midrange-plus-performance option for users in scientific
visualization and some of the more demanding MCAD and GIS users.

High-end 3D

Finally, for users requiring the fastest graphics available, the Freedom
Series graphics, designed and manufactured by Evans & Sutherland, are the
solution. Freedom Series graphics will be especially popular with users in
science and research. The Evans & Sutherland Freedom Series consists of
three models. The models 3150, 3250, and 3400 offer high-end 3D performance
for scientific visualization users and researchers in many industries. The
Freedom series model 3400 provides the fastest 3D graphics available in the
industry. These graphics, combined with the AlphaStation 600 workstation
yields the most powerful single processor graphics workstation available.
Freedom Series graphics is available on Digital UNIX systems only.

Graphics programming interfaces

These graphics options are supported by a wide variety of graphics
application programming interfaces (APIs). For 2D work, Digital's strategic
API on OpenVMS and Digital UNIX is the X Window System, including Xlib and
Motif. On Microsoft Windows NT, the strategic 2D API is Win32. For 3D work,
the strategic API is OpenGL which is supported on Digital UNIX, OpenVMS, and
Microsoft Windows NT. On OpenVMS and Digital UNIX, these APIs are made
available via DEC Open3D. Digital also supports PHIGS, PEX, and GKS as 3D
APIs on Digital UNIX and OpenVMS.

