Video Capture Options and Desktop Camera for AlphaStation Systems

HIGHLIGHTS

*  State-of-the art full motion video capture and video output PCI options
*  Independent of installed graphics subsystem, single slot PCI cards, for
   unlimited graphics and multimedia flexibility
*  Desktop compact color CCD camera for desktop video conferencing, video
   mail, and other simple capture

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

Digital's video capture options for AlphaStation systems--FullVideo Supreme,
FullVideo Supreme JPEG, and FullVideo Basic--are enabling technologies that
add video capture and live playback to all applications across industries,
including engineering, education, science, manufacturing, and healthcare.
Horizontal applications such as collaborative conferencing, e-mail,
presentations, on-line help, and databases significantly enhance
productivity when combined with video.

FullVideo Supreme, FullVideo Supreme JPEG, and FullVideo Basic capitalize on
the fast PCI bus in AlphaStation systems which accommodates large volumes of
data in support of the requirements of full motion video. All three options
can be added to any AlphaStation system and used with all supported
AlphaStation graphics subsystems.

When added to any of these options, the color CCD camera from Toshiba adds
video input capabilities for use with video conferencing, video capture,
multimedia authoring, and video mail to the system. These products expand
AlphaStation platforms to create rich multimedia environments across all
industries.

FullVideo Supreme and FullVideo Supreme JPEG

Designed to take advantage of AlphaStation system performance, FullVideo
Supreme and FullVideo Supreme JPEG are used to capture audio/video (AVI) and
video (YUV) files. FullVideo Supreme was Digital's first PCI video board for
AlphaStation workstations. Originally announced in April 1995 for Digital
UNIX (formerly called DEC OSF/1), FullVideo Supreme is now available for the
Windows NT and OpenVMS operating systems.

FullVideo Supreme JPEG is Digital's highest quality PCI video board for
AlphaStation systems, delivering full motion video input and output using
hardware assisted realtime JPEG compression. FullVideo Supreme JPEG is
supported under Windows NT, OpenVMS, and Digital UNIX.

Like the FullVideo Basic product, both boards are graphics independent and
can be used with 8 or 24 bit graphic adapters (8,16, and 24 bit supported on
Windows NT). FullVideo Supreme JPEG and FullVideo Supreme support full size
(NTSC 640 x 480) and full motion (30 frames per second) capture. Digital's
patented AccuVideo rendering technology provides superb video playback
quality on typical 8-bit graphics subsystems.

Through Multimedia Services V1.6 for Digital UNIX and Multimedia Services
V1.4 for OpenVMS, an application can incorporate audio and video data types
and features by using the capabilities of FullVideo Supreme, coupled with
the stereo quality audio that ships with all AlphaStation systems.

Features of these products are:

*  Full-motion video input in NTSC, PAL, and SECAM in both composite and
   S-video formats (composite only for FullVideo Supreme)
   - 30 frames per second NTSC, 640 x 480 - 50 fields per second PAL, SECAM,
     768 x 288
*  NTSC or PAL video output is provided in composite or S-video format
   (composite only for FullVideo Supreme) to record data from video input
   and/or graphics display to videotape, writeable optical disc, or display
   to a TV monitor (not available for Windows NT)
   - NTSC is supported in full frame output - PAL and SECAM are supported in
     field output only
*  Video can be captured and displayed on the workstation screen in the
   following modes:
   - 16-bit YUV (also known as YUV 4:2:2 mode) - 8-bit pseudo-color - 8-bit
     greyscale - 24-bit true color (with software assistance).
*  Independent of installed graphics subsystem (single slot PCI cards),
   allowing complete flexibility in the breadth of Digital's graphics
   offerings from 2D to high-end 3D. The frame-buffer free design helps
   integrate video into a standard X-based or Windows NT operating
   environment.
*  AccuVideo provides best-in-class video rendering (patent pending) in
   hardware, with superior perceptual image quality and flexibility with a
   choice in the number of output colors, brightness, contrast, saturation,
   and image sharpening.
*  Digital noise filtering in hardware not only improves the quality of the
   displayed image, but also improves the compression and scaling of images.
*  Video scaling maps video to window size from icon to full screen size.
   Video windows can be sized dynamically.
*  Two FullVideo Supreme or FullVideo Supreme JPEGs may be supported on one
   workstation for two simultaneous video inputs (not available for Windows
   NT) or one active video input and one active video output.
*  FullVideo Supreme JPEG includes these connectors:
   - RCA composite video input
   - RCA composite video output
   - S-video input
   - S-video output
* FullVideo Supreme includes these connectors:
   - RCA composite video input
   - RCA composite video output

FullVideo Basic

The FullVideo Basic option, licensed from ATI Technologies, Inc., is a
low-cost PCI video input option for AlphaStation systems running Windows NT.
FullVideo Basic takes advantage of AlphaStation system performance by
capturing a variety of video (YUV) and graphic (RGB) file formats, which are
desired for feeding MPEG, H.261, and Indeo software codecs. Supporting YUV
software compression is necessary when capturing and creating audio/video
files. In the near future, FullVideo Basic will also be supported under
Digital UNIX. Product features are:

*  Lowest cost video capture option for Windows NT on AlphaStation systems
*  Full-motion video input in NTSC and PAL in composite or S-Video format
   - 15 frames per second NTSC, 320 x 240
   - 30 fields per second NTSC, 320 x 120
   - 12 frames per second PAL, 384 x 288
   - 25 fields per second PAL, 384 x 144
*  ATI TV application for easily capturing still images
*  MEDIACAPTURE application for capturing and editing full motion NTSC or
   PAL video
*  Windows NT driver
*  Single slot PCI card independent of installed graphics subsystem for
   unlimited graphics and multimedia flexibility
*  Captures a variety of YUV and RGB formats
   - YUV12 (4:1:1) planar
   - YUV9 planar
   - YUV4:4:8, Y8 (mono),
   - RGB 8/16/24-bit
   - Dithered 8-bit mode
*  Allows capture of alternating fields as different formats (i.e.; even
   field capture as YUV, odd field capture as RGB)
*  FullVideo Basic includes these connectors:
   - 2 RCA composite video inputs
   - 1 S-Video input

Performance considerations

Performance when displaying live video through any of these video capture
boards or capturing video to disk is highly dependent on a number of system
features. For example, displaying live video on the monitor is highly
dependent on the speed of the PCI bus and the type of graphics card in the
system. Refer to the "Midrange 3D Graphics Accelerators for AlphaStation
Systems" article in this issue for specific performance numbers for the
various graphics options. In general, all AlphaStation workstations with the
ZLXp-E1 graphics option can display at least one 640 x 480 size image at 30
frames per second using the FullVideo Supreme and FullVideo Supreme JPEG
products.

Capturing live video from either option to disk is highly dependent on the
speed of the I/O subsystem. Capturing uncompressed data puts a much greater
strain on a system compared to capturing compressed data. For example,
approximately 9 frames per second of 320 x 240 uncompressed video may be
captured to a RZ25 disk on an AlphaStation system. Performance can be
greatly improved by using multiple disks in a stripe set

These video capture options, combined with InSoft's Communique! and the
desktop camera, are ideal for low-cost LAN-based video conferencing
solutions. Using InSoft's software CellB compression/decompression, two
AlphaStation users can expect video conferencing at 15 to 20 frames per
second (320 x 240), depending on system performance.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

FullVideo Supreme, FullVideo Supreme JPEG, and FullVideo Basic are available
in August 1995; the desktop camera will be available in July 1995.

